  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1216 AM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThings turn a bit more interesting for western to north central NM\nSaturday into Sunday as the remnants of Rachel and the\naforementioned eastern Pacific cold core low merge offshore of the\nBaja and then move into southern CA and AZ. The deepest moisture\nwill be drawn into the lower Colorado river basin between CA and\nAZ with more subdued and modified moisture coming into NM (PWATs\nrising to 0.8 to 1.0 inch). Stiffer westerly flow aloft will\narrive Saturday (15 to 25 kt at 500 mb), turning more\nsouthwesterly and increasing more on Sunday (25 to 50 kt at 500\nmb). With shortening day length and a lower solar elevation \nangle, diurnal destabilization is getting harder to come by, and \nmodel projections of instability are quite modest (just a couple\nto a few hundred joules of surface-based CAPE). This coupled with\nthe quick steering flow would likely keep storms spotty to \nscattered over the western half of the forecast area with some \norographics helping regenerate showers and storms over optimally \nnorthwest-to-southeast oriented slopes. Overall QPF is quite \nlight with most of these areas projected to receive 0.25 inch or \nless through Monday morning. \n\nBy Monday and Tuesday another deeper cold core low will be\ndropping into southern CA while the remnants of the next tropical\nstorm, Simon, get absorbed into the warm sector ahead of it via \nsouth southwesterly flow. There are a lot of uncertainties still \nwith the track and pace of both the cold core low and Simon, so \npinpointing how and if the moisture will stay intact is a bit \ndaunting this far out. The 00Z deterministic runs of the GFS and \nECMWF continue to align subtropical moisture and forcing aloft, \nbringing fairly widespread rain for much of NM Monday and Tuesday \nbefore transitioning to northern NM as cold core low dynamics \ntakeover into early Wednesday. The ensemble means are of course \nmore subdued with the PWATs, but still show some ranges in values \nfrom northwest to southeastern zones (0.7 to 1.2 inch) with the \nGEFS running on the higher side, not only on PWATs but also on \nQPF. For now, official QPF is generally staying between 0.1 to 0.5\ninches on Monday, rising to a 0.25 to 1.0 inch range on a more \nwidespread basis for Tuesday.",
